## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates expertise in designing schema evolution and tenant isolation in Azure data pipelines while balancing flexibility with data integrity.

## Key Concepts

- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Per-tenant isolation in Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2
- Lightweight schema registry design
- Data quality gates and validation strategies
- Migration and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate and route incoming record by tenant/version
async function processRecord(record, tenantId) {
  const schema = await getSchema(tenantId);
  const validationResult = validateAgainstSchema(record, schema);
  if (!validationResult.valid) {
    await sendToDeadLetterQueue(record, validationResult.errors);
    return;
  }
  await writeToDeltaLake(record, tenantId, schema.version);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle breaking changes across tenants?
- What monitoring alerts would indicate schema drift or validation failures?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-region streaming, real-time analytics, security, and data sovereignty in a practical Zoom-scale scenario.

## Key Concepts

- MongoDB Atlas Change Streams to Azure Event Hubs
- Azure Databricks Spark Structured Streaming
- Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2
- Synapse for analytics and dashboards
- BYOK with Azure Key Vault
- EU residency and cross-region DR

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: subscribe to MongoDB change stream and forward to Event Hub
const { MongoClient } = require('mongodb');
const { EventHubProducerClient } = require('@azure/event-hubs');
async function streamChanges(uri, db, coll, ehConnectionString, ehName){
  const client = new MongoClient(uri);
  await client.connect();
  const collObj = client.db(db).collection(coll);
  const cursor = collObj.watch();
  const producer = new EventHubProducerClient(ehConnectionString, ehName);
  for await (const change of cursor) {
    await producer.sendBatch([{ body: change }]);
  }
}
```

